@subject acffa2
@chapter 8
@title Intangible Assets
@lo D3
@topic t1 | Definition and recognition | D3
@topic t2 | Research vs development | D3
@topic t3 | Amortisation and subsequent measurement | D3

=== Q001
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which item is an intangible asset?
+ A purchased patent || Identifiable, non-monetary, no physical form.
- A delivery van || Tangible.
- Cash at bank || Monetary.
- Inventory of finished goods || Tangible and current.
explain: Intangibles must be identifiable and controlled.
mistake: Including monetary items.
tip: No physical form, not cash.
src: Study text ch8 - definition (concept basis; original question)

=== Q002
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: How is RESEARCH expenditure treated under IAS 38?
+ Always expensed to profit or loss as incurred || Benefits are too uncertain.
- Always capitalised || Not allowed.
- Capitalised if the criteria are met || That is development.
- Credited to equity || Not correct.
explain: Only development spending can be capitalised.
mistake: Capitalising research.
tip: Research = expense.
src: Study text ch8 - research (concept basis; original question)

=== Q003
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: If development expenditure meets ALL the IAS 38 criteria, what must the company do?
+ Capitalise it as an intangible asset || Capitalisation is required.
- Choose whether to capitalise it || Not optional.
- Expense it || Not when criteria are met.
- Credit it to revenue || Not income.
explain: Before all criteria are met, the spend is expensed.
mistake: Thinking capitalisation is optional.
tip: All criteria met = must capitalise.
src: Study text ch8 - development (concept basis; original question)

=== Q004
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: Capitalised development costs of $480,000 are amortised straight-line over 5 years with nil residual value. What is the annual amortisation ($)?
answer: 96000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Amortisation = (cost - residual value) / useful life
step: 480,000 / 5.
step: = $96,000.
check: 480000/5
explain: Amortisation is charged to profit or loss.
mistake: Starting before production begins.
tip: Cost / life.
src: Study text ch8 - amortisation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q005
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which item can NOT be recognised as an intangible asset?
+ Internally generated goodwill || Not identifiable or reliably measurable.
- A purchased licence || Recognised.
- A purchased trademark || Recognised.
- Development costs meeting all criteria || Recognised.
explain: Internally generated brands and customer lists are also excluded.
mistake: Recognising internal goodwill.
tip: Home-grown goodwill = no asset.
src: Study text ch8 - recognition (concept basis; original question)

=== Q006
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: When does amortisation of capitalised development costs begin?
+ When the product or process is available for use and commercial production begins || Benefits start.
- When development spending starts || Too early.
- When research starts || Research is expensed.
- At the end of the product's life || Too late.
explain: Amortisation spreads cost over the period of benefit.
mistake: Amortising during development.
tip: Start with sales.
src: Study text ch8 - amortisation timing (concept basis; original question)

=== Q007
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What does the "T" stand for in the PIRATE criteria for capitalising development costs?
+ Technically feasible || The project can be completed.
- Taxable || Not a criterion.
- Tangible || Intangibles are not tangible.
- Timely || Not a criterion.
explain: PIRATE: probable benefits, intention, resources, ability, technical feasibility, expenditure measurable.
mistake: Inventing criteria.
tip: Can it actually be built?
src: Study text ch8 - PIRATE (concept basis; original question)

=== Q008
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: What does "identifiable" mean for an intangible asset?
+ It is separable or arises from contractual or legal rights || One of the key features.
- It can be seen and touched || That is tangible.
- It is listed in the phone book || Not the meaning.
- It has been revalued || Not the meaning.
explain: Control and future benefits are the other features.
mistake: Equating identifiable with physical.
tip: Separable or legal right.
src: Study text ch8 - identifiable (concept basis; original question)

=== Q009
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: A patent is purchased for $50,000 and gives protection for 10 years. What is the annual amortisation ($)?
answer: 5000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Amortisation = cost / useful life
step: 50,000 / 10.
step: = $5,000.
check: 50000/10
explain: The legal life often limits the useful life.
mistake: Not amortising patents.
tip: Cost / years of benefit.
src: Study text ch8 - patents (concept basis; original question)

=== Q010
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: When can an intangible asset be measured using the revaluation model?
+ Only when fair value can be measured by reference to an active market || Rare for intangibles.
- Whenever the directors choose || Needs an active market.
- Always, for patents and brands || These rarely have active markets.
- Never || Possible in limited cases.
explain: Most intangibles use the cost model.
mistake: Revaluing brands.
tip: Active market needed.
src: Study text ch8 - revaluation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q011
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: During the year a company spent: research $40,000; development before all criteria were met $25,000; development after all criteria were met $90,000.
q: How much ($) should be capitalised as an intangible asset?
answer: 90000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Capitalise only development costs incurred after ALL criteria are met
step: Research 40,000 - expensed.
step: Development before criteria 25,000 - expensed; after criteria 90,000 - capitalised.
check: 90000
explain: Total expensed = 65,000.
mistake: Capitalising earlier development spend.
tip: From the date all criteria are met.
src: Study text ch8 - capitalisation (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q012
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Research $40,000; development before all criteria met $25,000; development after all criteria met $90,000.
q: How much is charged to profit or loss for the year (before amortisation)?
+ $65,000 || 40,000 + 25,000.
- $40,000 || Omits early development.
- $155,000 || Expenses everything.
- $90,000 || That is capitalised.
formula: Expense = research + development before criteria met
step: Research 40,000.
step: Plus early development 25,000 = $65,000.
check: 40000+25000
explain: Expensed development cannot be reinstated later.
mistake: Expensing only research.
tip: Everything before PIRATE is met.
src: Study text ch8 - expense (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q013
type: mr
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO are among the criteria that must be met before development costs are capitalised?
+ Adequate resources are available to complete the project || Resources.
+ The expenditure can be measured reliably || Expenditure.
- The project has already made a profit || Not required.
- The research phase cost more than $100,000 || Not a criterion.
explain: All six PIRATE criteria must be met.
mistake: Adding profit tests.
tip: PIRATE, all of it.
src: Study text ch8 - criteria (concept basis; original question)

=== Q014
type: match
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Match each item with its accounting treatment.
pair: Research into new materials => Expense as incurred
pair: Development meeting all criteria => Capitalise as intangible asset
pair: Development before criteria were met => Expense as incurred
pair: Internally generated brand => Not recognised as an asset
explain: Only qualifying development is capitalised.
mistake: Capitalising internally generated brands.
tip: Research and brands = no asset.
src: Study text ch8 - treatments (concept basis; original question)

=== Q015
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: Development costs of $120,000 were capitalised. Commercial production began on 1 October and the product's life is 5 years. The year end is 31 December. What amortisation ($) is charged this year (pro rata)?
answer: 6000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Amortisation = cost / life x months / 12
step: Annual = 120,000 / 5 = 24,000.
step: 3 months = 24,000 x 3 / 12 = $6,000.
check: 120000/5*3/12
explain: Amortisation starts when production begins.
mistake: Charging a full year.
tip: From the start of production.
src: Study text ch8 - pro rata (concept basis; original question)

=== Q016
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which activity is RESEARCH rather than development?
+ Searching for new scientific knowledge about a material's properties || Original investigation.
- Building and testing a prototype of a new product || Development.
- Designing tools for a new product line || Development.
- Testing pre-production models || Development.
explain: Development applies knowledge to specific products or processes.
mistake: Classing prototypes as research.
tip: Knowledge = research; product = development.
src: Study text ch8 - research vs development (concept basis; original question)

=== Q017
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: Development costs of $240,000 are amortised over 6 years from the start of production. What is the carrying amount ($) after 2 full years?
answer: 160000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Carrying amount = cost - accumulated amortisation
step: Annual amortisation = 240,000 / 6 = 40,000; 2 years = 80,000.
step: Carrying amount = 240,000 - 80,000 = $160,000.
check: 240000-240000/6*2
explain: The cost model is cost less amortisation and impairment.
mistake: Forgetting accumulated amortisation.
tip: Cost less amortisation to date.
src: Study text ch8 - carrying amount (concept basis; original question)

=== Q018
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: How is the annual amortisation of an intangible asset recorded?
+ Dr amortisation expense; Cr accumulated amortisation || Like depreciation.
- Dr intangible asset; Cr amortisation || Reversed.
- Dr retained earnings; Cr asset || Not standard.
- No entry || An entry is required.
explain: The expense is charged to profit or loss.
mistake: Debiting the asset.
tip: Same as depreciation.
src: Study text ch8 - amortisation entry (concept basis; original question)

=== Q019
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Development costs were expensed in Year 1 because the criteria were not met. In Year 2 the criteria are met. Can the Year 1 costs be capitalised?
+ No - costs expensed in earlier periods cannot be reinstated || Only future costs.
- Yes - they must be reinstated || Not allowed.
- Yes - if the directors choose || Not allowed.
- Only if the product is profitable || Not allowed.
explain: Capitalisation applies from the date the criteria are met.
mistake: Reinstating earlier costs.
tip: No going back.
src: Study text ch8 - reinstatement (concept basis; original question)

=== Q020
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: How is an intangible asset with an INDEFINITE useful life treated?
+ It is not amortised but is tested for impairment || No amortisation.
- It is amortised over 50 years || Not required.
- It is expensed immediately || Not correct.
- It is revalued annually || Not required.
explain: Indefinite does not mean infinite; it means no foreseeable limit.
mistake: Amortising regardless.
tip: Indefinite = impairment tests.
src: Study text ch8 - indefinite life (concept basis; original question)

=== Q021
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: This year: research costs $30,000 and amortisation of previously capitalised development costs $20,000. New development spending of $70,000 meets all criteria.
q: What is the total charge ($) to profit or loss for research and development?
answer: 50000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: P/L charge = research expensed + amortisation
step: Research 30,000.
step: Plus amortisation 20,000 = $50,000 (the new $70,000 is capitalised).
check: 30000+20000
explain: The new spend will be amortised once production starts.
mistake: Expensing qualifying development.
tip: Research + amortisation.
src: Study text ch8 - P/L charge (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q022
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: What are the conditions for recognising any intangible asset?
+ Future economic benefits are probable and cost can be measured reliably || General recognition criteria.
- It must be physically separable || Not required.
- It must have been revalued || Not required.
- It must be bought for cash || Not required.
explain: Internally generated goodwill fails these tests.
mistake: Adding extra conditions.
tip: Probable benefits + reliable cost.
src: Study text ch8 - recognition (concept basis; original question)

=== Q023
type: mcq
level: S
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
q: A trainee capitalises $35,000 spent on a laboratory study to discover new chemical compounds. What is wrong?
+ This is research and must be expensed || Original investigation.
- Nothing is wrong || It is research.
- It should be revalued || Not relevant.
- It should be credited to revenue || Not income.
explain: Profit and assets are overstated by $35,000.
mistake: Capitalising research.
tip: Discovery = research.
src: Study text ch8 - research (concept basis; original question)

=== Q024
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Which is a required IAS 38 disclosure?
+ The total research and development expenditure expensed in the period || Required.
- The names of the research staff || Not required.
- The future selling price of the product || Not required.
- The patent application number || Not required.
explain: Useful lives, methods and a reconciliation of carrying amounts are also required.
mistake: Thinking no disclosure is needed.
tip: Show what was expensed.
src: Study text ch8 - disclosure (concept basis; original question)

=== Q025
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Why are internally generated brands not recognised as assets?
+ Their cost cannot be distinguished reliably from the cost of developing the business as a whole || Not reliably measurable.
- Because brands have no value || They can be valuable.
- Because IAS 38 bans all brands || Purchased brands are recognised.
- Because brands are tangible || They are intangible.
explain: A purchased brand has a reliable cost.
mistake: Recognising home-grown brands.
tip: Bought yes; built no.
src: Study text ch8 - internal brands (concept basis; original question)

=== Q026
type: order
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Put the stages of a product's development in the order they are usually accounted for.
item: Research costs expensed as incurred
item: Development costs expensed until all criteria are met
item: Development costs capitalised once all criteria are met
item: Amortisation begins when commercial production starts
explain: This reflects increasing certainty of future benefits.
mistake: Amortising before production.
tip: Expense, expense, capitalise, amortise.
src: Study text ch8 - sequence (concept basis; original question)

=== Q027
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Project costs this year: January-April (criteria not yet met) $36,000; May-December (all criteria met from 1 May) $64,000.
q: What is the closing intangible asset ($), assuming production has not yet started?
answer: 64000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Intangible = development costs incurred after criteria met
step: January-April 36,000 - expensed.
step: May-December 64,000 - capitalised; no amortisation yet.
check: 64000
explain: Amortisation begins only when production starts.
mistake: Amortising before production.
tip: Capitalise from the date criteria are met.
src: Study text ch8 - capitalisation (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q028
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which feature distinguishes an intangible asset from property, plant and equipment?
+ It has no physical substance || Key difference.
- It is always current || Usually non-current.
- It cannot be amortised || It usually is.
- It is never purchased || Many are purchased.
explain: Both are non-current assets used in the business.
mistake: Thinking intangibles are current.
tip: No physical form.
src: Study text ch8 - comparison (concept basis; original question)

=== Q029
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Under the cost model, how is an intangible asset measured after recognition?
+ Cost less accumulated amortisation and impairment losses || Standard model.
- Fair value at each year end || Revaluation model.
- Original cost forever || Ignores amortisation.
- Selling price || Not used.
explain: Most intangibles use the cost model.
mistake: Ignoring amortisation.
tip: Cost less amortisation.
src: Study text ch8 - cost model (concept basis; original question)

=== Q030
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which criterion is NOT met if the company has decided to abandon the project?
+ Intention to complete and use or sell the asset || Intention is missing.
- Technical feasibility || May still be feasible.
- Expenditure measured reliably || May still be measurable.
- Resources available || May still be available.
explain: Without intention, the spend is expensed.
mistake: Capitalising abandoned projects.
tip: No intention = no asset.
src: Study text ch8 - criteria (concept basis; original question)

=== Q031
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Capitalised development costs of $300,000 relate to a product launched on 1 July 20X5, with an expected life of 4 years. The year end is 31 December.
q: What is the carrying amount at 31 December 20X6?
+ $187,500 || 18 months' amortisation of 112,500.
- $225,000 || Only 1 year deducted.
- $150,000 || 2 full years deducted.
- $300,000 || No amortisation.
formula: Carrying amount = cost - cost / life x months / 12
step: Annual = 300,000 / 4 = 75,000; months since launch = 18.
step: Amortisation = 75,000 x 18/12 = 112,500; carrying amount = 300,000 - 112,500 = $187,500.
check: 300000-75000*18/12
explain: Pro rata amortisation applies from the launch date.
mistake: Counting full years.
tip: Count months from launch.
src: Study text ch8 - carrying amount (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q032
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Intangible asset b/f: $180,000. This year: research $55,000; qualifying development $95,000; amortisation charge $36,000.
q: What is the intangible asset c/f ($)?
answer: 239000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: C/f = b/f + qualifying development - amortisation
step: 180,000 + 95,000 = 275,000.
step: 275,000 - 36,000 = $239,000.
check: 180000+95000-36000
explain: Research is expensed and never enters the asset.
mistake: Adding research.
tip: Only qualifying development goes in.
src: Study text ch8 - movements (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q033
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t2
status: review
q: A company is developing a new battery. It has proven the technology works, plans to sell it, has funding and can measure costs. But market research shows no customers are likely to buy it. Should the costs be capitalised?
+ No - probable future economic benefits are not shown || One criterion fails.
- Yes - five out of six criteria are met || All must be met.
- Yes - technical feasibility is enough || Not enough.
- Only half the costs || Not an option.
explain: All PIRATE criteria are required.
mistake: Capitalising when one criterion fails.
tip: Six out of six.
src: Study text ch8 - criteria (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q034
type: mr
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO are recognised as intangible assets?
+ A licence bought to broadcast sports events || Purchased right.
+ A customer list purchased from another company || Purchased and identifiable.
- An internally generated customer list || Not recognised.
- Staff training costs || Not controlled.
explain: Employees are not controlled assets.
mistake: Capitalising training.
tip: Purchased rights qualify.
src: Study text ch8 - examples (concept basis; original question)

=== Q035
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t3
status: review
q: A trainee starts amortising development costs from the date spending began, two years before the product was launched. What is the effect?
+ Amortisation is charged too early, understating profit and the asset before launch || Should start at production.
- No effect || Timing matters.
- Profit is overstated || Understated.
- Only the notes are affected || Figures are affected.
explain: Benefits begin with commercial production.
mistake: Amortising during development.
tip: Launch starts amortisation.
src: Study text ch8 - timing (concept basis; original question)

=== Q036
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: A company capitalised $150,000 of development costs. Production began on 1 April 20X7; expected life 5 years; year end 31 December.
q: What is the amortisation charge ($) for the year ended 31 December 20X7?
answer: 22500
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Charge = cost / life x months / 12
step: Annual = 150,000 / 5 = 30,000.
step: 9 months = 30,000 x 9 / 12 = $22,500.
check: 150000/5*9/12
explain: April to December is 9 months.
mistake: Charging a full year.
tip: Count the months.
src: Study text ch8 - pro rata (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q037
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Why is the revaluation model rarely used for intangible assets?
+ Most intangibles, such as patents and brands, are unique and have no active market || Fair value not reliable.
- IAS 38 prohibits it entirely || Allowed with an active market.
- Intangibles never change in value || They can.
- Revaluation increases tax || Not the reason.
explain: Taxi licences or quotas sometimes have active markets.
mistake: Thinking revaluation is common.
tip: Unique = no active market.
src: Study text ch8 - revaluation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q038
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Spending on a new process: feasibility research $18,000; prototype building before funding was secured $42,000; prototype testing after all criteria were met $56,000; staff training for the launch $9,000.
q: How much ($) should be capitalised?
answer: 56000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Capitalise only qualifying development spend
step: Research 18,000 and pre-criteria prototype 42,000 are expensed.
step: Training 9,000 is expensed; capitalise 56,000.
check: 56000
explain: Training costs are not part of the intangible asset.
mistake: Capitalising training.
tip: Only post-criteria development.
src: Study text ch8 - capitalisation (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q039
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t3
status: review
q: A product using capitalised development costs is withdrawn from the market after one year because of a safety problem. What is the likely treatment?
+ The remaining carrying amount is written off as an impairment, as no future benefits are expected || Asset no longer supported.
- Continue amortising over the original life || No benefits remain.
- Transfer it to inventory || Not inventory.
- Reinstate it as research || Not correct.
explain: Assets cannot be carried above their recoverable amount.
mistake: Leaving the asset in place.
tip: No benefits = write off.
src: Study text ch8 - impairment (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q040
type: match
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Match each PIRATE letter with its criterion.
pair: P => Probable future economic benefits
pair: I => Intention to complete and use or sell
pair: R => Resources available to complete
pair: A => Ability to use or sell the asset
explain: T = technical feasibility; E = expenditure measured reliably.
mistake: Forgetting a criterion.
tip: Spell PIRATE.
src: Study text ch8 - PIRATE (concept basis; original question)

=== Q041
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Development costs capitalised: Project A $200,000 (launched 1 January 20X8, life 4 years); Project B $90,000 (not yet launched). Year end 31 December 20X8.
q: What is the total amortisation charge ($) for 20X8?
answer: 50000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Charge only launched projects
step: Project A: 200,000 / 4 = 50,000.
step: Project B is not launched: no amortisation; total $50,000.
check: 200000/4
explain: Unlaunched projects are assets but not yet amortised.
mistake: Amortising Project B.
tip: Launched only.
src: Study text ch8 - amortisation (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q042
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
q: A finance director says, "Our development costs meet every criterion, but we will expense them to be prudent." Is this acceptable?
+ No - IAS 38 requires capitalisation when all criteria are met || Not optional.
- Yes - prudence overrides IAS 38 || Not correct.
- Yes - capitalisation is optional || Required.
- Only for small companies || No exemption.
explain: Expensing understates assets and profit.
mistake: Treating capitalisation as optional.
tip: Criteria met = must capitalise.
src: Study text ch8 - compulsory capitalisation (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q043
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which of the following BEST explains "control" of an intangible asset?
+ The entity can obtain the future benefits and restrict others' access, often through legal rights || Key feature.
- The entity has physical possession || Not needed.
- The entity's staff created it || Not enough.
- The entity has insured it || Not the test.
explain: Skilled staff are not controlled, so training is not an asset.
mistake: Treating staff skills as assets.
tip: Benefits + restrict access.
src: Study text ch8 - control (concept basis; original question)

=== Q044
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: A licence costing $72,000 with a 6-year life was purchased 2.5 years ago. What is its carrying amount ($) under the cost model?
answer: 42000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Carrying amount = cost - cost / life x years elapsed
step: Annual = 72,000 / 6 = 12,000; 2.5 years = 30,000.
step: Carrying amount = 72,000 - 30,000 = $42,000.
check: 72000-72000/6*2.5
explain: Purchased licences are amortised over their useful life.
mistake: Forgetting the half year.
tip: Count time precisely.
src: Study text ch8 - licences (concept basis; original question)

=== Q045
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Why is research expensed rather than capitalised?
+ At the research stage, future economic benefits cannot be shown to be probable || Too uncertain.
- Because research is always cheap || Not the reason.
- Because research is a tangible asset || It is not.
- Because tax rules demand it || Not the reason.
explain: Development is closer to a commercial outcome.
mistake: Thinking it is about amount.
tip: Too early to know.
src: Study text ch8 - research (concept basis; original question)

=== Q046
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t1
status: review
q: A company spends $500,000 on advertising to build its brand. How is this treated?
+ Expensed as incurred; internally generated brands are not recognised || No asset.
- Capitalised as a brand intangible || Not allowed.
- Capitalised as development || Not development.
- Treated as inventory || Not inventory.
explain: Only a purchased brand would be recognised.
mistake: Capitalising advertising.
tip: Advertising = expense.
src: Study text ch8 - brands (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q047
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Research $28,000; development before criteria met $15,000; development after criteria met $60,000; amortisation of earlier development $12,000.
q: What total amount ($) is charged to profit or loss?
answer: 55000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Charge = research + early development + amortisation
step: 28,000 + 15,000 = 43,000.
step: Plus amortisation 12,000 = $55,000.
check: 28000+15000+12000
explain: The $60,000 is capitalised.
mistake: Expensing qualifying development.
tip: Expense everything except qualifying development.
src: Study text ch8 - P/L charge (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q048
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Over what period should capitalised development costs be amortised?
+ The period over which the product or process is expected to generate benefits || Useful life.
- Always 5 years || No fixed period.
- Always 20 years || No fixed period.
- The period of development || Too early.
explain: Useful lives are reviewed regularly.
mistake: Using a fixed rule.
tip: Life of the benefits.
src: Study text ch8 - useful life (concept basis; original question)

=== Q049
type: mcq
level: F
cat: revision
topic: t2
status: review
important: 1
q: Which statement BEST summarises IAS 38 for research and development?
+ Research is always expensed; development is capitalised when all PIRATE criteria are met || Core rule.
- Both are always capitalised || Research is expensed.
- Both are always expensed || Development can be capitalised.
- Development is optional to capitalise || Required.
explain: Capitalised costs are amortised from launch.
mistake: Treating capitalisation as optional.
tip: Research out; qualifying development in.
src: Study text ch8 - R&D (concept basis; original question)

=== Q050
type: mcq
level: S
cat: revision
topic: t1
status: review
important: 1
q: What is the IAS 38 definition of an intangible asset in simple terms?
+ An identifiable asset that is not money and has no physical form || Core definition.
- Any asset that is hard to value || Not the definition.
- Any expense the directors choose to capitalise || Not allowed.
- A current asset || Usually non-current.
explain: Recognition also needs probable benefits and reliable cost.
mistake: Recognising internal goodwill.
tip: Identifiable, non-monetary, non-physical.
src: Study text ch8 - definition (concept basis; original question)
